\section{Analysis: Mechanisms of Compound Severity}
\label{sec:analysis}

The results in \S\ref{sec:results} reveal a striking asymmetry: importance-based eviction exhibits $69$--$274{\times}$ severity amplification in low-sparsity phases (Table~\ref{tab:main-results}), while random eviction remains flat at ${\sim}1{\times}$ across all phases. We investigate two candidate mechanisms for this emergent phase-strategy interaction: KV discrimination collapse, which we find robust across both models, and attention entropy redistribution, which proves model-dependent.

\subsection{KV Discrimination Collapse}
\label{sec:discrimination-collapse}

The KV importance score (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance}) aggregates attention mass across surviving heads to determine which cache entries to evict. We measure the discriminative power of these scores using the Gini coefficient of the per-position importance distribution: higher Gini indicates greater concentration of importance mass on a few positions, enabling reliable identification of eviction candidates. The coefficient of variation (CV) provides a complementary measure of score dispersion.

Under compound sparsity, we observe consistent Gini degradation across both models, all sparsity levels, and both eviction strategies. For importance-based eviction, the Gini delta ranges from $-0.013$ to $-0.038$ on DeepSeek and $-0.010$ to $-0.020$ on Qwen---all negative, indicating that compound conditions uniformly reduce the discriminative power of importance scores. Random eviction shows comparable degradation (DeepSeek: $-0.020$ to $-0.052$; Qwen: $-0.019$ to $-0.045$). The CV confirms the same directional pattern: all conditions show negative CV deltas, with Qwen exhibiting smaller absolute magnitudes.

This discrimination collapse is the most robust finding in our mechanistic analysis: across two models $\times$ two eviction strategies $\times$ four sparsity levels, all 16 conditions show consistent Gini reduction. When importance scores become more uniform, thresholding the bottom-$s$ fraction becomes noise-sensitive---small perturbations change which positions cross the eviction boundary. In the limit, importance-based eviction degenerates into an arbitrary selection functionally equivalent to biased random eviction.

This mechanism directly accounts for the crossover phenomenon (\S\ref{sec:crossover}). In high-sparsity phases, importance scores retain sufficient discrimination despite Gini reduction, and importance-based eviction removes genuinely less critical positions. In low-sparsity phases, where attention is already broadly distributed, the additional Gini reduction pushes importance scores toward uniformity, and eviction devolves into a biased pseudo-random process that can inadvertently remove critical positions---producing higher compound severity than the explicitly random baseline. The severity inversion is thus not a failure of the importance metric in isolation, but an emergent consequence of the interaction between head pruning and importance scoring conditioned on the phase-dependent attention structure.

Random eviction is immune to this mechanism: its decisions are independent of attention patterns and invariant to the redistribution caused by head pruning, explaining the flat severity gradient ($G \approx 1$) at all sparsity levels.

\subsection{Attention Entropy Redistribution}
\label{sec:attn-redistribution}

We further examine attention entropy redistribution as a secondary mechanism. When attention heads are pruned, surviving heads compensate for lost information pathways, potentially altering the entropy of their attention distributions:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}_i^{(h)} = -\sum_{j=1}^{T} a_{ij}^{(h)} \log a_{ij}^{(h)}
    \label{eq:attn-entropy}
\end{equation}

On DeepSeek, importance-based eviction produces consistent negative entropy deltas across all sparsity levels ($\Delta\mathcal{H}$: $-0.040$ at $s{=}0.15$, $-0.047$ at $s{=}0.20$, $-0.045$ at $s{=}0.25$, $-0.044$ at $s{=}0.30$; cross-problem standard deviation $0.02$--$0.03$), indicating a systematic and low-variance redistribution effect. Random eviction, in contrast, shows consistently positive entropy deltas ($+0.04$ to $+0.08$), confirming that the sign of redistribution depends on the eviction strategy.

On Qwen, the pattern only partially replicates. While $s{=}0.15$ and $s{=}0.20$ show negative entropy deltas ($-0.073$ and $-0.076$ respectively), $s{=}0.25$ exhibits a sign reversal to $+0.045$, and $s{=}0.30$ approaches zero ($-0.007$). The reversal at $s{=}0.25$ is systematic rather than driven by outliers: 22 of 30 problems show positive entropy deltas at this sparsity level. Cross-problem standard deviations on Qwen are $4$--$6{\times}$ larger than on DeepSeek ($0.08$--$0.16$ vs.\ $0.02$--$0.03$), indicating substantially higher heterogeneity in Qwen's response to compound sparsity.

One potential confound is statistical power in the mid-sparsity phase: Qwen produces fewer reasoning segments than DeepSeek (26 vs.\ ${\sim}50$ median segments at $s{=}0.25$), which could inflate variance estimates. However, the $s{=}0.25$ entropy reversal is consistent across all three phase categories (low-sparsity phase: $+0.042$, mid-sparsity: $+0.034$, high-sparsity: $+0.017$), which does not match the prediction that reduced statistical power in mid-phase alone drives the divergence. We therefore report statistical power as one contributing confound, but acknowledge that the reversal likely also reflects genuine model-specific differences in how Qwen's attention heads respond to compound pruning---potentially arising from its instruction-tuning regime versus DeepSeek's distillation-based training.

Random eviction entropy deltas on Qwen are consistently positive ($+0.08$ to $+0.22$), paralleling DeepSeek's direction with larger magnitude, consistent with Qwen's generally higher sensitivity to perturbation.

\subsection{Integrated Mechanism}
\label{sec:integrated-mechanism}

Evidence suggests that compound severity amplification arises from the interaction of two factors operating at different levels of robustness.

\paragraph{KV discrimination collapse (robust).} Head pruning reduces the discriminative power of importance scores, as measured by consistent Gini and CV degradation across all 16 tested conditions (two models $\times$ two strategies $\times$ four sparsity levels). This effect is phase-modulated: in low-sparsity phases where attention is already broadly distributed, the marginal discrimination loss has disproportionate impact on eviction quality, explaining the severity amplification concentrated in these phases.

\paragraph{Entropy redistribution (model-dependent).} On DeepSeek, head pruning systematically redistributes attention entropy under importance-based eviction (consistent negative deltas, low variance), providing a coherent secondary pathway through which pruning degrades eviction quality. On Qwen, this effect is present at 2 of 4 sparsity levels but reverses at $s{=}0.25$, suggesting that model-specific factors---potentially differences in attention head specialization arising from distillation versus instruction tuning---modulate the manifestation of this mechanism.

\paragraph{Architecture generality.} The discrimination collapse mechanism depends on two properties shared by all attention-mass-based KV eviction methods---H$_2$O \cite{h2o2024}, SnapKV \cite{snapkv2024}, PyramidKV \cite{pyramidkv2024}: (i)~importance scoring derived from attention weights, and (ii)~heterogeneous attention concentration across reasoning phases. We therefore expect phase-modulated compound severity to emerge whenever head pruning is combined with attention-based cache eviction. Importance metrics derived from sources independent of attention mass---such as activation magnitude or gradient-based saliency---would not be subject to the same collapse and should produce flatter severity gradients; this prediction is directly testable. The model dependence of entropy redistribution suggests, however, that the magnitude and detailed structure of the severity gradient may vary across model families.

\paragraph{Reframing compound severity.} The flat random baseline ($G \approx 1$) demonstrates that phase-modulated severity amplification is not an inherent cost of compound sparsity \emph{per se}. Rather, it is an emergent phase-strategy interaction: discrimination collapse under compound conditions degrades importance-based eviction preferentially in phases where attention is broadly distributed. This reframes compound severity as a targetable phenomenon that a phase-aware sparsity controller could, in principle, mitigate by adjusting the eviction budget or strategy per phase.

\paragraph{Effect sizes.}
Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d} reports Cohen's $d$ (bootstrap $B{=}10{,}000$) measuring the standardized difference in II between low- and high-sparsity phases under importance-based eviction.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
